  "query_preview": "..\n\nBlocking pattern will result in surface high pressure and ridging \nbeing entrenched across the Great Lakes yielding quiet weather \nconditions through the week. \n\nA surface high pressure will move across the northern Great Lakes \ninto Quebec tonight and tomorrow. Light southwesterly winds today \nwill become more east-northeasterly for tomorrow while remaining \nlight. This shift in winds brings the only notable weather item for \nthis forecast, which will be the potential for some isolated areas \nof patchy shallow fog that may develop with the help of the lake \nmoisture. \n\nThermal profiles over the course of the week will not change all \nthat much with a persisting warmer airmass that brings temperatures \nthat are a few degrees above climatological norms. Look for \nafternoon highs mainly in the 60s with overnight lows ranging from \nthe mid-upper 30s to mid 40s. \n\nThe active northern stream across central Canada will attempt to \nsend a front into the region. It should weaken as it progresses and \nonly manage to reach the northern Lakes before longwave ridge \namplification ramps back up ensuring continued dry and slightly \nabove normal warmth through the end of the week. High pressure \ncentered over eastern Canada will offer potential for slightly \ncooler temperatures Friday down to 60 with the northerly flow. \nTemperatures quickly recover as thermal ridge builds back into the \nGreat Lakes for the weekend.",
